## Solution

We evaluate the regularized integral
$$I(\epsilon) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} t\, e^{-2it}\, e^{-\epsilon t^2}\, dt, \qquad \epsilon > 0.$$

### Step 1: Fourier transform of the Gaussian

Recall the standard result (obtained by completing the square):
$$F(\omega) := \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-\epsilon t^2}\, e^{-i\omega t}\, dt = \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-\omega^{2}/(4\epsilon)}, \qquad \epsilon > 0.$$

### Step 2: Differentiate under the integral sign

Since $e^{-\epsilon t^2}$ makes the integrand rapidly decaying, we may differentiate with respect to $\omega$ under the integral:
$$F'(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-\epsilon t^2}\,(-it)\, e^{-i\omega t}\, dt = -i\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} t\, e^{-\epsilon t^2}\, e^{-i\omega t}\, dt.$$

On the other hand, differentiating the closed form:
$$F'(\omega) = \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\cdot\left(-\frac{\omega}{2\epsilon}\right) e^{-\omega^{2}/(4\epsilon)} = -\frac{\omega}{2\epsilon}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-\omega^{2}/(4\epsilon)}.$$

Equating and solving for the desired integral:
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} t\, e^{-\epsilon t^2}\, e^{-i\omega t}\, dt = \frac{1}{-i}\, F'(\omega) = \frac{1}{i}\cdot\frac{\omega}{2\epsilon}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-\omega^{2}/(4\epsilon)} = -\frac{i\omega}{2\epsilon}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-\omega^{2}/(4\epsilon)}.$$

### Step 3: Evaluate at $\omega = 2$

Setting $\omega = 2$:
$$I(\epsilon) = -\frac{2i}{2\epsilon}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-4/(4\epsilon)} = -\frac{i}{\epsilon}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{\epsilon}}\, e^{-1/\epsilon} = -i\sqrt{\pi}\, \epsilon^{-3/2}\, e^{-1/\epsilon}.$$

### Step 4: Take the limit $\epsilon \to 0^{+}$

We need $\lim_{\epsilon \to 0^+} \epsilon^{-3/2} e^{-1/\epsilon}$. Substituting $u = 1/\epsilon \to +\infty$:
$$\epsilon^{-3/2} e^{-1/\epsilon} = u^{3/2}\, e^{-u} \xrightarrow{u\to\infty} 0,$$
since the exponential decay $e^{-u}$ dominates any polynomial growth $u^{3/2}$.

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\lim_{\epsilon \to 0^{+}} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} t\, e^{-2it}\, e^{-\epsilon t^{2}}\, dt = 0.}$$

This is consistent with the distributional interpretation: $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} t\, e^{-i\omega t}\, dt = 2\pi i\,\delta'(\omega)$, and $\delta'$ is supported at $\omega = 0$, so evaluating at $\omega = 2$ (away from the support) gives $0$.

